  "_style": "Write natural, warm Afaan Oromoo in Latin script (Qubee), never Ge'ez/Fidel. Always address the user in the singular “ati” (kee, si, siif, sitti) and use singular imperatives for buttons and actions (“Olkaa'i”, “Ergi”, “Irra deebi'ii yaali”); for the couple together use “isin”/“lamaan keessan” and first-person plural “nu/keenya” when the couple speaks (“Oddoo keenya”, “Muka keenya”). The other person is always “jaalallee kee” (gender-neutral); never use dhirsa/haadha manaa/intala/gurbaa. Oromo verbs mark 3rd-person gender (inni/isheen, -e/-te), so avoid 3rd-person verbs about the partner: rephrase with second person, the passive or impersonal (-ame, -ama: “Xalayaan siif ergameera”), a noun phrase (“Ergaa jaalallee keetii”), or a relative “kan …” clause. A partner-name placeholder (%@) is never declined: never attach case suffixes, focus or conjunction suffixes to it (%@n, %@f, %@tti, %@'s, %@iis); keep postpositions as separate words (“%@ irraa”, “%@ wajjin”, “%@ biratti”) or rephrase (“Jaalallee kee — %@”). Use curly quotes “ ” (inner ‘ ’); the hudhaa (glottal stop) is part of the word and is always the straight apostrophe ' (U+0027), never ’ or omitted (“har'a”, “deebi'i”); double vowels and doubled consonants must be kept exactly (“muka” vs “mukka” matter). Ellipsis is the single character “…”; no space before . , ? ! : ; keep “ · ”, \\n, emoji and placeholders unchanged and in a natural position (Oromo is verb-final, reorder freely). Numbers: digits as in source, decimal point “.” and thousands separator “,”; dates day/month/year and month names in Oromo (Amajjii, Guraandhala, Bitootessa, Ebla, Caamsaa, Waxabajjii, Adooleessa, Hagayya, Fulbaana, Onkoloolessa, Sadaasa, Muddee) when spelled out. Titles, tabs and buttons in sentence case (only the first letter capitalized) except proper names: Coupley, Premium, CoupleSync, AI (“AI” stays as is). Plural categories are exactly [\"one\",\"other\"]: “one” uses the singular noun (“guyyaa %lld”), “other” the plural or numeral form (“guyyoota %lld”); if a language tool emits extra categories, give them the same text as “other”. Tone: kind, light, close, like a good Oromo app — never cheesy, never word-for-word.",
